# Bayesian Coin: KDE posterior vs Beta (conjugate) — README

This README explains the nonparametric **KDE-style posterior** implementation you added (file: `stats_analyzer/bayesian_kde_coin.py`) and compares it to the classic **Beta conjugate prior** approach (file: `stats_analyzer/bayesian_coin.py`).

It focuses on intuition, when to use each approach, how the KDE posterior is computed, and how to run and tune the code.

---

## TL;DR

* **Beta (conjugate) posterior**: analytic, exact for Binomial/Bernoulli models. Very fast and requires only counts of heads/tails. Use when model assumptions (Bernoulli trials) hold and you want closed-form updates.

* **KDE-style posterior (nonparametric)**: compute posterior on a grid (`prior(p) * likelihood(data | p)`), then smooth the grid with a Gaussian kernel to produce a continuous posterior estimate. Use when you want a flexible posterior (custom priors, multimodal shapes, or visual exploration) and are willing to trade compute for flexibility.

